@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' SPEC source (the logic this diagram tracks):
'   internal/workspace/mount.go — MountSpec.Plan, linkMounts, refuseLinkTarget, escapingEnvFile
'   cmd/proveo/main.go          — reportLinks (operator-facing record of each decision)
'   internal/wsscan/wsscan.go   — AlwaysPrune, the shared deny set the walk reuses
title Symlinks that leave the mount — a bind mount carries the link's text, not its meaning

node "Host — mount planning, before the container exists" as HOST <<host>> {
  component "MountSpec.Plan()\nper-layout mount model" as PLAN <<app>>
  component "linkMounts(hostDir,\ncontainerBase, ro)" as WALK <<app>>
  component "refuseLinkTarget(target, root)\nthe two guardrails" as GUARD <<app>>
  component "wsscan.AlwaysPrune\nshared deny set" as PRUNE <<app>>
  component "envMounts · maskEnvMounts\nescapingEnvFile — the credential policy" as ENV <<app>>
  component "reportLinks\n[]Link → screen + transcript" as REPORT <<app>>
}

database "Workspace tree\ndepth <= 3, pruned" as TREE <<db>>
database "Escape target\noutside the mounted tree" as OUT <<db>>

node "Container runtime" as RT <<host>> {
  component "runc — resolves a mount\ndestination THROUGH the symlink" as RUNC <<app>>
  component "/workspace/input/_spec\nstill a symlink, now resolvable" as SEEN <<app>>
}

component "Dangling link inside the container\nempty directory · no error · feature absent" as DEAD <<error>>

PLAN ARROW_MAIN WALK : once per bind-mounted tree
WALK ARROW_MAIN TREE : WalkDir, never follows a link
WALK ARROW_MAIN PRUNE : same answer as every other walk
WALK ARROW_MAIN GUARD : may this target be mounted?
WALK ARROW_OPTIONAL ENV : dotenv is not ours to place
WALK ARROW_MAIN OUT : mount target AT the link's own container path
OUT ARROW_MAIN RUNC
RUNC ARROW_MAIN SEEN : materialises the target path in the rootfs
WALK ARROW_MAIN REPORT : every decision, never silent
GUARD ARROW_ERROR DEAD : refused — the link stays broken, with a reason
PLAN ARROW_ERROR DEAD : without this pass, EVERY escaping link ends here

note right of DEAD
  **The failure this exists to remove.** A bind mount copies a symlink's
  //text//. ""_spec -> ~/specs/pluvo"" — the idiom for carrying scaffolding a
  repo must not commit — arrives in the container pointing at a path that
  does not exist there. ""ls _spec/"" reports an empty directory, nothing
  logs a warning, and the feature the link carries is simply gone.

  It bit this repo twice. An ad-hoc ""_spec"" symlink made the spec tooling
  unavailable inside the agent, and ""\.env -> ~/base\.env"" printed
  ""No \.env found"" in the ""input-output"" layout, which had no overlay to
  fall back on the way ""app"" does.
end note

note bottom of RUNC
  **Why mounting at the link's own path works.** The destination is
  resolved through the symlink inside the container's mount namespace, so
  the target path is created in the rootfs and the link resolves — while
  staying a link. Reads //and// writes land in the real host directory,
  which is what lets ""spec-keeper"" edit a symlinked ""_spec"".

  Nothing in the workspace is rewritten. Repointing the link in place
  would mutate the operator's own checkout and is impossible anyway when
  the tree is mounted ""ro"". Copying the target in would drift and lose
  write-back. Docker has no follow-symlinks bind mode to ask for.

  An absolute escape materialises the host-absolute path inside the
  container; a relative one (""\.\./shared/x"") lands under the mount's own
  parent. Both resolve. The link mount inherits the workspace's ""ro""
  mode, never widening it.
end note

note right of WALK
  **The case matrix.**

  | link resolves to | disposition |
  | inside the mounted tree | nothing — it already works, and saying so buries the rest |
  | outside, absolute | mounted at the link's container path |
  | outside, relative | mounted (destination resolves under the mount) |
  | nowhere on the host | refused — broken before proveo saw it |
  | a path containing the workspace | refused |
  | a top-level system directory | refused |
  | a dotenv secrets file | left to the credential policy |
  | past the 32-mount cap | refused, and counted out loud |
end note

note bottom of PRUNE
  **Bounded three ways, for one reason.** Depth stops at 3, ""AlwaysPrune""
  drops installed and generated trees, and 32 mounts cap docker's argv.

  Escaping links are a scaffolding idiom — ""_spec"", ""docs"", ""\.env"" — that
  lives at or near the root. Walking a six-figure monorepo once per run to
  find one is the cost that made LSP detection unusable (see
  ""_lsp_walk"" in ""packages/lib/entrypoint-lib\.sh""), and this walk runs in
  front of an operator waiting for a container to start.

  ""WalkDir"" does not follow symlinks, so a link to a directory arrives as
  an entry that is never descended into: no cycle is reachable however
  the link points.
end note

note bottom of GUARD
  **Two rules, about scale rather than taste.** The operator wrote the
  link themselves, inside their own repo, so planning honours an explicit
  local instruction — the guardrails exist to stop a catastrophic one.

  //Target contains the workspace// rejects ""/"", a home directory holding
  the checkout, and the monorepo root above a subdir scope: mounting it
  would nest the tree inside itself and hand back the scope the run was
  narrowed to. //Top-level system directory// rejects a single-segment
  target (""/etc"", ""/usr"", ""/Users"", ""/home"").

  **Documented limit:** a two-segment target elsewhere on the disk is
  allowed, and on macOS ""/etc"" resolves to ""/private/etc"" and passes the
  second rule. The first rule still covers every case that matters,
  because a repo under the operator's home makes that home a container of
  the workspace.
end note

note right of ENV
  **Dotenv is owned by the credential policy, not by link resolution.**
  Isolating egress masks every secrets file with ""/dev/null""; a forwarding
  run overlays the resolved host file. Both decide what belongs at that
  container path, and a second mount on the same destination is a docker
  error rather than a fallback — so the walk records the link and places
  nothing.

  ""escapingEnvFile"" closes the ""input-output"" gap: it overlays the
  resolved target only when the path IS a symlink leaving the tree. A real
  file there already works, and mounting it over itself would add a
  redundant ""-v"".
end note

note bottom of REPORT
  **A mounted link widens what the sandbox can reach**, so it is named on
  screen rather than applied quietly: one line per link, target included,
  teed into the run transcript. A refused link is the line the operator
  came looking for — the answer to "why is my ""_spec"" empty" — and the
  cap refuses out loud rather than truncating in silence.
end note
@enduml
